Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-20-2000||11:18 AM||||Is the engine missing at idle? or is the idle rpm acting erratically?<P>I've heard of guys in the forum using the Bosch +4 plugs in the 2.8 with no problems, but haven't used them myself.<P>Let us know so we can help you out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||09-20-2000||01:08 PM||||sorry, my fault.  it is idling at 1500 rpm and jumps to 2000 and back to 1500 rpm.  it seems like it's buring really rich.  btw: what is the best way to adjust the timming, we followed the haynes manual way (grounding the computer and then setting the timming, which is what it says on the underside of the trunk lid), but i also saw in the chiltons and clymer manuals to unplug the connector at the distributor.  does this do the same thing?  thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||09-22-2000||04:14 PM||||well, we replaced the bosch +4s with ac delcos and it was idling just fine... until the next day. now it won't even start.. like the timming in totally off again!!!  grr.  we know that the oxygen sensor needs to be replaced, but that wouldn't keep it from starting right would it?  (at least it's not my car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||09-22-2000||04:28 PM||||The O-2 sensor will not prevent starting. Double check the timing to make sure your not a tooth off, then check the fuel pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||09-22-2000||07:44 PM||||Setting the timing on a Fiero is an art in itself...something to do w/ adding #'s,then subtracting..blah blah blah..I usually do it by ear, seems to work.<BR>You also may want to check your IAC..could need replacing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||KnightRyder31||09-23-2000||07:22 PM||||My fiero's idle is acting up also. When I start her up, it revs up to 2K, then to 1K...but then it swoops below 1K and she feels like she's trying to stall. When I'm at the light, it stays at 1K, then swoops below 1K, and acts like she wants to stall. WTF? Maybe O2 Senser? I don't know...help.||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||09-23-2000||07:42 PM||||btoth: Yes, when it comes to setting ignition timing, grounding the diagnostic terminal does the same thing as unplugging the connector at the distributor.  What it does is, it keeps the ECM from adjusting the timing.  I usually do it by grounding the diagnostic terminal, because I like to check for error codes at the same time, plus, less chance of damaging/ wearing out the connector on the distributor.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sabre||09-24-2000||06:19 PM||||KnightRyder31 : Exact same thing here... in fact my 2.8 V6 does the same thing.<P>Installed a complete Accel ignition system, check for vacum leaks, and had the fuel pump tested.. NO LUCK.<P>Mine is also not that easy to start... some times requires a 2nd crank.<P>If anyone can help please do, I've been trying to fix this for years.<P><BR>||||reg||5
